**DISAGREE as written. Keep the paired rule, but call its result a practical lead, give the middle outcome an honest name, and explicitly record which existing release requirements the council is changing.** On 20 matters, four served only by the new system and none served only by the old would pass the practical rule. Even assuming independent matters, the one-sided sign-test probability would be 0.0625. That is a lead worth showing Alex. [Proposed paired rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-10/claude-1.md:10)

1. **Write the full verdict before the first send.** Use the proposed rule for a practical lead and its mirror for a practical loss. Require the result to hold under both split countings. A guardrail breach confirmed under both countings is **Worse**. If a split could change the verdict, or neither side meets the margin, say **No practical winner**. “No worse” would claim more than this rule tests. Fix the denominators and minimum count for each guardrail subgroup. An empty or undersized subgroup cannot clear a guardrail. Keep D428’s original limits visible so the new 10-point guardrail does not silently replace them. [Paired rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-10/claude-1.md:10) · [D428](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4756)

2. **Show the sign test and interval for what they measure.** The sign test asks whether the new system wins more often among matters served by only one side. Even a result below 0.05 does not establish a 10-point gain across all matters. The ordinary exact calculation also assumes independent discordant matters. Freeze how related matters and weeks are grouped. If the design cannot support that assumption or a defensible interval that accounts for the grouping, label the statistics illustrative. Keep **20 distinct matters and 30 distinct questions per system per draw** as minimum counts for the practical reading. Neither my earlier 30-week illustration nor the “about half the time” estimate establishes power for this paired measure. [Sizing assumptions and limits](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/comparison/COMPARISON-SIZING.md:45)

3. **Freeze the population and how a matter earns credit.** Use July 1–19 to build both libraries, then score July 20–September 20 only if the full exposure census supports that span. D613 establishes the 10 and 4 exposures for the weeks of September 7 and 14 from Step 1 traces. Freeze the actual exclusion list and apply it to both replays. Fix one scoring cutoff per matter, how repeat mentions are grouped, and what counts as the same question. A missing question or answer earns no credit. Hash the article list, versions, reader instructions, controls, and verdict rule before sending. Keep Alex’s spot checks beside the prewritten verdict. [D613](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:8197) · [Source-first reading and denominators](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/ACCEPTANCE-DESIGN.md:21) · [Reader protocol](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/pilot-deck-1/council-marking/PROTOCOL.md:24)

4. **Record the authority separately.** The absolute floors were proposals. D427 and D431 were agreed with Alex and include the frozen-reference comparison and Step 2’s three checks. If the council wants the frozen-reference result reported alongside the comparison, say explicitly what D664 supersedes and retain the Step 2 checks unless that requirement is expressly changed. Likewise, moving line 6 to code should preserve the agreed **90%** due-day consistency threshold unless the decision explicitly changes it. D658 waits. Even a practical lead goes to Alex before any release engineering resumes. [D400](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4381) · [D427](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4738) · [D431](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4795) · [D664](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:9383)
